    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: does this choice actually make France more capable of acting without depending on any single ally's decision, or does it just look that way. Gaullist strategic autonomy is the foundational instinct, expressed at its most concentrated through the dissuasion nucléaire doctrine and the deliberately undefined intérêts vitaux, an ambiguity that is itself the doctrine's whole point, and the domaine réservé is the institutional space that lets a French president pursue this vision even amid domestic political turbulence, cohabitation included. The UN Security Council seat and the Indo-Pacific presence France's overseas territories provide are the concrete global levers this autonomy actually runs through, permanent, sovereign, and beholden to no ally's continued cooperation, and Francophonie is the cultural and linguistic soft-power network running alongside them, tied to language and colonial history rather than to military or economic weight alone. EU leadership ambitions scale the identical Gaullist logic up to the European level, France's bet that a strategically autonomous Europe amplifies rather than dilutes French influence, while the AUKUS crisis shows the real cost this posture can carry when it collides directly with an ally's own interests, a reminder that autonomy protects France from depending on others even as it leaves France exposed when others decide independently of French interests in turn. Diplomatie économique is the machinery built to convert all of these relationships into commercial advantage, and the Françafrique legacy, finally, shows this entire global vision now being actively renegotiated, and in several cases forcibly ended, at its historical African foundation, precisely where French influence has traditionally run deepest and now faces its sharpest rejection. As you follow any real French foreign-policy decision from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and ask first whether Paris is genuinely acting alone here, or whether that appearance of autonomy is quietly more fragile, and more dependent on relationships France cannot fully control, than it looks.",
