      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: can the government actually count to a majority here, or does today's fragmented arithmetic mean it has to reach for one of this system's other, more improvisational tools instead. Fait majoritaire and the groupes parlementaires a government now actually has to court together set the negotiating environment every other concept in this lecture operates within, and Article 49.3, together with the precise motion-de-censure arithmetic and the vote bloqué, are the tools a government reaches for once ordinary vote-by-vote negotiation cannot reliably produce passage. The navette and the commission mixte paritaire show how a bill reconciles two different chambers' versions once it has actually passed the Assembly, and ordonnances bypass this entire apparatus for defined policy domains a government can negotiate authority over just once. Ad hoc, issue-by-issue bargaining is the fragmented era's genuine, improvisational alternative to Germany's stable coalition-agreement model, and the Barnier government's December 2024 fall is this lecture's clearest proof of what happens when every one of these tools finally fails at once. As you follow any real French legislative fight from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and ask first whether the government can actually count to 289, or is quietly hoping it will not have to.",
